{"name":"Grand Duke","hex":"#cb5c45","fetched":"2026-10-12","looked":{"definitions":[{"term":"grand duke","whole":true,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["Ruler or monarch of a grand duchy.","Son, daughter, or grandchild of a czar (emperor) of the Russian Empire. (A more literal translation of the Russian is grand prince.)"]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/grand_duke"}],"entries":[{"title":"Grand duke","blocks":[{"text":"Grand duke is a European hereditary title, used either by certain monarchs or by members of certain monarchs' families. The title is used in some current and former independent monarchies in Europe titled as Grand Duchy, particularly:"},{"items":["in present-day Luxembourg, the last territory ranked as Grand Duchy","historically by the sovereigns of former independent countries, such as Tuscany","in Baden, Hesse, Oldenburg, Mecklenburg-Schwerin, Mecklenburg-Strelitz and Saxe-Weimar-Eisenach – grand duchies from 1815 to 1918, and all now part of Germany","formerly also in some countries in Northern Europe, such as the Grand Duchy of Finland or the Grand Duchy of Lithuania","currently by claimants to the Russian Imperial Throne"]}],"link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Grand_duke","wikidata":"Q205706"},{"title":"Duke","blocks":[{"text":"Duke is a male title either of a monarch ruling over a duchy, or of a member of royalty, or nobility. As rulers, dukes are ranked below emperors, kings, grand princes, grand dukes, and above sovereign princes. As royalty or nobility, they are ranked below grand dukes and above or below princes, depending on the country or specific title. The title comes from French duc, itself from the Latin dux, 'leader', a term used in republican Rome to refer to a military commander without an official rank, and later coming to mean the leading military commander of a province. In most countries, the word duchess is the female equivalent."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/e/ec/James_Brydges%2C_1st_Duke_of_Chandos_by_Michael_Dahl.jpg/330px-James_Brydges%2C_1st_Duke_of_Chandos_by_Michael_Dahl.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Duke","wikidata":"Q166886"}],"images":[{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/5/51/StampFinland1860Scott5.jpg/250px-StampFinland1860Scott5.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/5/51/StampFinland1860Scott5.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail_unscaled 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/5/51/StampFinland1860Scott5.jpg/250px-StampFinland1860Scott5.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"1860 postage stamp of the Grand Duchy of Finland","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3AStampFinland1860Scott5.jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/c/c1/%D0%9B%D0%B5%D0%B2_%D0%94%D0%B0%D0%BD%D0%B8%D0%BB%D0%BE%D0%B2%D0%B8%D1%87.jpg/500px-%D0%9B%D0%B5%D0%B2_%D0%94%D0%B0%D0%BD%D0%B8%D0%BB%D0%BE%D0%B2%D0%B8%D1%87.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/c/c1/%D0%9B%D0%B5%D0%B2_%D0%94%D0%B0%D0%BD%D0%B8%D0%BB%D0%BE%D0%B2%D0%B8%D1%87.jpg/500px-%D0%9B%D0%B5%D0%B2_%D0%94%D0%B0%D0%BD%D0%B8%D0%BB%D0%BE%D0%B2%D0%B8%D1%87.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"Portrait of Leo I, King of Ruthenia and Grand Duke of Kiev (18th-century painting)","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3A%D0%9B%D0%B5%D0%B2_%D0%94%D0%B0%D0%BD%D0%B8%D0%BB%D0%BE%D0%B2%D0%B8%D1%87.jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/f/f3/King_Vitoldo.png/500px-King_Vitoldo.png?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/f/f3/Ki